> # llama.cpp fork: 1.7x to 2.4x faster decode on MoE models bigger than your VRAM

The newest mixture-of-experts models (GLM-5.3-Flash, MiMo-V2.6-Flash, Qwen3.8-Flash-Next, Qwen3.6) are far bigger than a gaming GPU.
This fork keeps their experts in RAM and turns the free VRAM into a live cache of the experts the model is using; the GPUs compute
the cached ones and the CPU the rest, at the same time. No special switches needed.

```sh
llama-server -m GLM-5.3-Flash-GSQ-RCO-3.0bit-q4kattn.gguf
llama ... (truncated)
Thank you for providing the information about the llama.cpp fork with mixture-of-experts models (MoE) that fit in GPU VRAM. Here are the key findings and the comparison with the stock llama.cpp:

1. **Decode speedup**: The largest gain in performance is achieved when approximately one-third to one-half of the MoE model fits in GPU VRAM (1.9x to 2.4x). The decode speedup is the largest when the model size is between 1.5GB and 2.5GB.

2. **Gain over stock**: The gain in performance over stock llama.cpp is the share of the model that fits in GPU VRAM. The table below shows the gain for each model on each machine:

   | model on machine | share of the model in VRAM | gain |
   |---|---|---|
   | MiMo IQ3_XXS 132 GB on A

